            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Emigration and International Success</strong><br>\nAfter the 1917 Russian Revolution, Rachmaninoff emigrated to the United States. He achieved considerable success as a concert pianist, which was necessary to support his family financially since the revolution had stripped them of their estate. Although he composed less frequently in America, his works from this period, such as the \"Rhapsody on a Theme of Paganini,\" remained popular.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Composer as Performer</strong><br>\nRachmaninoff was one of the last great virtuosos who was also a composer. His extensive touring schedule as a pianist often eclipsed his composing career. However, his piano playing was renowned for its expressiveness, precision, and clarity, which were characteristics that also defined his compositions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Musical Style and Influences</strong><br>\nRachmaninoff's musical style was deeply rooted in the Romantic tradition, with rich harmonies and expressive melodies. His works are characterized by their technical demands and emotional depth, often reflecting the influence of Tchaikovsky, Rimsky-Korsakov, and other Russian Romantic composers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
