          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rachmaninoff’s Works and Legacy</strong><br>\nRachmaninoff’s output includes symphonies, concerti, operas, and chamber music, but he is perhaps best remembered for his piano works, which have become staples of the repertoire. His legacy is preserved through numerous recordings, including those he made as a pianist, which provide insight into his interpretive style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Last Years and World War II</strong><br>\nRachmaninoff’s health declined during the last years of his life, yet he continued to perform and compose. He was affected by World War II, which broke out in his final years, limiting his opportunities to perform and affecting his mood and productivity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Enduring Popularity</strong><br>\nSergei Rachmaninoff died on March 28, 1943, in Beverly Hills, California, just four days before his 70th birthday. Today, he is celebrated for his enduring melodies and the emotional power of his music, which continues to resonate with audiences around the world, securing his place as one of the most loved of Russian composers.</p>\n"
              },
